# Capstone 4 – Manga Search Web Application

## 📌 Project Overview

Capstone 4 is a manga search web application developed as part of my full-stack web development learning journey.

The application integrates with the **MangaDex API** to retrieve manga information and dynamically display results based on user-selected search criteria.

This project focuses on working with external APIs, processing JSON data, dynamically rendering content with EJS, and creating structured webpage layouts using CSS Flexbox.

## 🛠️ Technologies Used

- **HTML5** – Structures the webpage, forms, and search results.
- **CSS3** – Provides custom styling and webpage layouts.
- **CSS Flexbox** – Organizes and aligns webpage elements using flexible containers.
- **JavaScript** – Handles application logic, array iteration, and data processing.
- **Node.js** – Provides the server-side JavaScript runtime.
- **Express.js** – Manages application routes and HTTP requests.
- **EJS (Embedded JavaScript)** – Dynamically generates HTML using server-provided data.
- **Axios** – Sends HTTP requests to the external API.
- **MangaDex REST API** – Provides manga information and search data.
- **JSON** – Used to process structured API response data.

## ✨ Features

- **Manga Search** – Retrieves manga information from the MangaDex API.
- **Dynamic Dropdown Menu** – Populates selection options using API-provided tag data.
- **Tag Selection** – Allows users to select a manga category or tag.
- **Selected Option Retention** – Keeps the selected dropdown option active when results are displayed.
- **Dynamic Search Results** – Displays manga titles retrieved from the API.
- **Conditional Rendering** – Displays results or error messages depending on application state.
- **Error Handling** – Displays an error message when one is provided by the server.
- **Flexbox Layout** – Uses CSS Flexbox to organize webpage elements.

## ⚙️ How It Works

### 1. Express Server

The application uses Node.js and Express.js to create a local web server.

```javascript
import express from "express";
import axios from "axios";

const app = express();
const port = 3000;
```

### 2. External API Integration

Axios communicates with the MangaDex API to retrieve manga information.

```javascript
const API_URL = "https://api.mangadex.org/manga";
```

The application processes API responses and passes the relevant data to EJS templates for display.

### 3. Dynamic EJS Templates

EJS allows JavaScript logic to run inside HTML templates.

The application uses:

- `forEach()` to iterate through arrays of manga and tag objects.
- `<%= %>` to display dynamic values within HTML.
- `<% %>` to execute JavaScript logic without directly displaying it.
- `<%# %>` to add EJS comments.
- `if / else if` statements to conditionally render content.

### 4. Dynamic Tag Selection

The application generates dropdown options from tag objects and retains the user's selected option.

```ejs
<% myArray.forEach(function(tag) { %>
  <option value="<%= tag.id %>"
    <%= (locals.selectedTag === tag.id)
      ? "selected" : "" %>>
    <%= tag.attributes.name.en %>
  </option>
<% }); %>
```

### 5. Displaying Manga Results

The application checks whether an error exists or manga results are available before rendering content.

```ejs
<% if (locals.error) { %>
  <p><%= error %></p>
<% } else if (locals.mangaList && mangaList.length > 0) { %>
  <ul>
    <% mangaList.forEach(function(manga) { %>
      <li>
        <%= Object.values(manga.attributes.title)[0] %>
      </li>
    <% }); %>
  </ul>
<% } %>
```

## 💻 Running the Project Locally

### 1. Clone the Repository

```bash
git clone https://github.com/lydiaperdomo/FullDev.git
```

### 2. Navigate to Capstone 4

```bash
cd FullDev/Capstone4
```

### 3. Install Dependencies

```bash
npm install
```

### 4. Start the Server

```bash
node index.js
```

Replace `index.js` with the actual server filename if different.

### 5. Open the Application

Navigate to:

`http://localhost:3000`

